    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Iranian-speaking horse nomads related to the Sarmatians, who dominated the steppe north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> from the 1st century CE.</li><li>Broken by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a> around 370, some Alans crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> in 406 and went on with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>.</li><li>Those who stayed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> formed the Kingdom of Alania, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ossetians">Ossetians</a> speak a language descended from theirs.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ammianus-marcellinus">Ammianus Marcellinus</a> wrote that the Alans worshipped a naked sword thrust into the ground as their god of war, and foretold the future with willow rods.</p></aside>
    </section>

