    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/josephus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/josephus.jpg" alt="Josephus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Joseph,_son_of_Gorian,_called_Flavius_Josephus_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Scan by NYPL, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Alans were an Iranian-speaking people of the steppes north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> and the Caspian, closely related to the Sarmatians and the earlier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a>. Their name is generally thought to derive from an old Iranian form related to <em>Aryan</em>. They appear in Greek and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> sources in the first century CE, when Roman writers such as Seneca, Lucan and the Jewish historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a> begin to mention them as a new and formidable power between the Don and the Caucasus. Chinese sources of the Han period refer to a people called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yancai">Yancai</a>, later renamed Alanliao, north-west of the Caspian, which many scholars connect with the rise of the Alans, although the identification is debated. The Alans probably formed as a new confederation within the Sarmatian world, absorbing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aorsi">Aorsi</a> and other groups and drawing in peoples of different origins. The historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ammianus-marcellinus">Ammianus Marcellinus</a>, writing in the late fourth century, describes how they had gradually overcome their neighbours and given them their own name, so that 'Alans' covered many peoples spread across the steppe from the Don eastwards. They were never a single state with fixed borders, but a shifting union of clans and chiefdoms bound by language, way of life and military success, whose leaders could combine for great raids and then disperse.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/rome"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rome.jpg" alt="Rome" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Trevi_Fountain,_Rome,_Italy_2_-_May_2007.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Diliff, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Alans lived as mounted pastoralists, moving with their herds of horses, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and sheep between seasonal pastures. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ammianus-marcellinus">Ammianus Marcellinus</a> describes them living in wagons covered with bark, which served as moving homes, and claims that they despised farming and lived on meat and milk; such portraits drew on Greek and Roman stereotypes of nomads, and archaeology shows that some Alan groups, especially in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-caucasus">North Caucasus</a> foothills, lived in settled villages and hillforts and practised agriculture. Society was organised in clans under chiefs, with a warrior elite whose wealth is visible in rich burials containing weapons, horse gear, gold ornaments and objects imported from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>. Ammianus reports that the Alans had no slaves and that all were of noble birth, another idealised claim, and that they valued death in battle above all. Their religion included the worship of a naked sword thrust into the ground, honoured as the god of war, and divination with willow rods. Trade with the Greek cities of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> and with Iran brought wine, metalwork and luxury goods in exchange for horses, livestock and captives. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> the Alans controlled the passes, above all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darial-pass">Darial Gorge</a>, which gave them leverage over the empires to the south.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-adrianople"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-adrianople.jpg" alt="Battle of Adrianople" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-adrianople">Battle of Adrianople</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Adrianople_378_en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Elias84; derivative work by Dipa_1965, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Alans fought chiefly as cavalry. Their nobles were heavily armoured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lance">lancers</a>, using long spears held with both hands, supported by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horse-archer">horse archers</a>, and Roman writers admired their skill and mobility. In the early 70s CE, according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a>, Alans crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> passes and raided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/media-region">Media</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, nearly capturing the Armenian king Tiridates. Around 135 another Alan invasion struck Albania, Media and Armenia; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arrian">Flavius Arrian</a>, governor of Cappadocia, prepared his army to meet them and wrote a short treatise, the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/order-of-battle-against-the-alans">Order of Battle against the Alans</a></em>, describing how Roman infantry should receive the charge of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heavy-cavalry">heavy cavalry</a>. Alans served as allies and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> of both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. Around 370 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a> defeated the Alans near the Don, and many Alans joined the Huns or moved west with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a>; Alan cavalry is reported among the Gothic forces at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-adrianople">Battle of Adrianople</a> in 378. On the last day of 406, according to the traditional date, Alans crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a> and Suebi, entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> and in 409 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a>. Others served Rome: Alans under Sangiban fought on the Roman side against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-catalaunian-plains">Battle of the Catalaunian Plains</a> in 451. In the sixth century the Caucasian Alans, under rulers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarosius">Sarosius</a>, were valued allies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ammianus-marcellinus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ammianus-marcellinus.jpg" alt="Ammianus Marcellinus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ammianus-marcellinus">Ammianus Marcellinus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ammianus_Marcellinus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Author of the original work of art is long dead, while the uploader of the picture portraying it released it under a CC BY 3.0 license., CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Alans had no permanent central government. Power lay with the chiefs of clans and larger groups, who could be elected or accepted as war leaders for a campaign, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ammianus-marcellinus">Ammianus</a> says that they chose as leaders men distinguished in war. Different Alan groups followed their own kings and pursued their own policies, which explains why, in the fifth century, Alans fought on both sides of many conflicts. In the west, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/respendial">Respendial</a> and Goar led separate groups across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> in 406, and while Respendial's Alans saved the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a> from defeat by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>, Goar went over to the Roman side and his followers were later settled near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a> by the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flavius-aetius">Aetius</a>; another group under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sambida">Sambida</a> was settled near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valence">Valence</a> around 440. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a> the Alans received Lusitania and Carthaginiensis in the division of 411, and their king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/addac">Addac</a> ruled the strongest of the invading groups until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> destroyed his army in 418. In the east, Alan rulers in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-caucasus">North Caucasus</a> negotiated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> and the steppe empires: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarosius">Sarosius</a> helped the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avar</a> envoys reach the Byzantine court around 558. By the seventh century the Alans of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> were tributary to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazar Khaganate</a>, and only gradually did a more centralised Kingdom of Alania emerge from their chiefdoms.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/aspar"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aspar.jpg" alt="Aspar" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/aspar">Aspar</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Piatto_di_ardaburio,_argento_fuso,_434_d.c._(found_in_1769)_03.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">sailko, CC BY 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Alans were among the most widely travelled peoples of late antiquity, and their history connects the steppe, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Roman Gaul</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>. Their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heavy-cavalry">heavy cavalry</a> influenced the military practice of their neighbours, and some historians have argued for a lasting Alan contribution to the cavalry of the late Roman and early medieval west, though the extent of this influence is debated. Alan soldiers and commanders rose high in Roman service; the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aspar">Aspar</a>, of Alan descent, was one of the most powerful men in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Eastern Roman Empire</a> in the mid-fifth century. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> the Vandal kings styled themselves kings of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a> and Alans until the Byzantine conquest of 533–534. In the Caucasus the Alans controlled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darial-pass">Darial Pass</a> and held the balance between Byzantium, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazars</a> and later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>, and their successor, the Kingdom of Alania, adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> in the early tenth century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ossetians">Ossetians</a> of the central Caucasus speak a language descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alanic-language">Alanic</a> and preserve the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nart-sagas">Nart</a> epic tradition, which shares motifs with older steppe lore. Groups of Alans also survived in medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jassic-people">Jász</a>, and in Mongol service, where an Alan guard served the Yuan emperors in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate.jpg" alt="Khazar Khaganate" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazar Khaganate</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chasaren.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Alans never fell as a single state, but their great confederation was broken by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a>. Around 370 the Huns defeated the Alans on the Don, killing many and forcing others to submit; Alan contingents then fought in Hunnic armies, while other groups fled west with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a>. In the west, the Alans who entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a> were gradually absorbed. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a>, acting for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, crushed the Alans of Hispania in 418, and the survivors placed themselves under the Vandal king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunderic">Gunderic</a> and crossed with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> in 429, where they merged into the Vandal kingdom. The Alans settled in Gaul by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flavius-aetius">Aetius</a> were assimilated into the Gallo-Roman and Frankish population, leaving traces mainly in place names. In the east, the Alans who remained in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-caucasus">North Caucasus</a> foothills survived as a people under their own chiefs, but from the seventh century they were tributary to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazar Khaganate</a>, while Arab armies campaigned through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> in the eighth century. From these chiefdoms the Kingdom of Alania took shape around the ninth century, the medieval successor of the Alans, which lasted until the Mongol invasions of the thirteenth century drove its people into the mountains.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Alans were a nomadic Iranian-speaking people whose cavalry dominated the steppe north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>, raided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, and after the Hunnic invasions scattered across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, while those who stayed in the Caucasus became the ancestors of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ossetians">Ossetians</a>.</em></p>

